    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Writing tools</p>
    <h1>What Is Markdown? A Simple Guide to Writing for the Web</h1>
    <p class="lead">Markdown is a way of writing formatted text using plain characters. A # makes a heading and asterisks make bold text, so you can write without leaving the keyboard.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The basic syntax</h2>
<ul>
<li><code># Title</code> makes a large heading, and <code>##</code> and <code>###</code> make smaller ones.</li>
<li><code>**bold**</code> makes bold text and <code>*italic*</code> makes italic text.</li>
<li><code>[link text](https://example.com)</code> makes a link.</li>
<li>A line starting with <code>-</code> is a bullet point, and a line starting with <code>1.</code> is a numbered item.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where it is used</h2>
<p>Markdown appears in README files on code websites, in note-taking apps, in documentation and in many blogging systems. The same text can be turned into HTML, so it fits well with websites.</p>
<h2>Small differences between tools</h2>
<p>Markdown has several versions. Some tools support tables, task lists or footnotes, and others do not. If a feature does not appear, check which flavour your tool uses.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/markdown-preview.html">Markdown Preview</a> shows the formatted result as you type. Nothing is saved or sent anywhere.</p>
